import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Compile a deterministic graph."
  description="Use StateKnot's implemented graph compiler to validate a bounded topology and freeze its schema, reducer, limits, and canonical digest."
  section="Compiled graphs"
>
  <aside class="docs-callout" aria-labelledby="graph-boundary-title">
    <h2 id="graph-boundary-title">
      Implemented compiler, not a runnable Agent loop
    </h2>
    <p>
      <code>CompiledGraph</code>, deterministic barrier planning, and the
      PostgreSQL pinned graph registry are implemented. Model/tool dispatch and
      the complete durable barrier driver are still active work, so this
      tutorial stops at the boundary the repository can execute today.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <section aria-labelledby="run-title">
    <h2 id="run-title">Run the maintained example</h2>
    <p>
      The example is compiled by the workspace's all-targets gate. It declares
      <code>validate → finish</code>, compiles the descriptor, and prints the
      SHA-256 digest of its canonical definition.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-code" data-code-block>
      <div class="docs-code-head">
        <span>Shell · from a repository checkout</span>
        <button
          class="copy-button affordance"
          type="button"
          data-copy-button
          data-copy-target="compiled-graph-command"
        >
          <span data-copy-label aria-live="polite">Copy</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <pre
        id="compiled-graph-command"
        tabindex="0"><code>cargo run -p stateknot-core --example compiled_graph --locked</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      Read the complete Apache-2.0 example in
      <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/crates/stateknot-core/examples/compiled_graph.rs"
        ><code>compiled_graph.rs</code></a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="contract-title">
    <h2 id="contract-title">What compilation freezes</h2>
    <ol class="evidence-list">
      <li>
        <h3>Owner-qualified identity</h3>
        <p>
          A graph name is local to an issuer/subject owner and an exact semantic
          version. Any definition change requires another version.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Schema and reducer references</h3>
        <p>
          Input, state, update, output, and reducer implementation references
          are digest-pinned; executable code is never inferred from a name.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Bounded topology</h3>
        <p>
          Entry nodes, successors, routes, maximum parallelism, and maximum
          supersteps are part of the immutable definition.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Canonical identity</h3>
        <p>
          Nodes and routes are put in stable order, serialized as canonical
          JSON, and hashed. Checkpoints retain the resulting exact reference.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="reject-title">
    <h2 id="reject-title">Invalid graphs fail before admission</h2>
    <p>The compiler rejects, among other cases:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>duplicate node or route identities;</li>
      <li>missing entry or successor nodes;</li>
      <li>nodes unreachable from every entry;</li>
      <li>paths that cannot reach a wait or terminal boundary;</li>
      <li>ready sets above graph parallelism limits; and</li>
      <li>definitions above the node, route, or two-MiB byte ceilings.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="registry-title">
    <h2 id="registry-title">Register once, recover by exact reference</h2>
    <p>
      Migration 13 stores canonical compiled definitions under a tenant plus
      owner/name/version key. Re-registering identical bytes is idempotent;
      reusing the version for another digest is a conflict. Recovery recompiles
      the bytes and validates the checkpoint binding under the live fence.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-code" data-code-block>
      <div class="docs-code-head">
        <span>Rust · current pre-alpha store API</span>
        <button
          class="copy-button affordance"
          type="button"
          data-copy-button
          data-copy-target="register-graph-code"
        >
          <span data-copy-label aria-live="polite">Copy</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <pre
        id="register-graph-code"
        tabindex="0"><code>let registered = store
    .register_graph_definition(tenant_id, graph)
    .await?;

let pinned = recovery.load_pinned_graph().await?;</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      Missing, altered, cross-tenant, or checkpoint-mismatched graph evidence
      fails closed. During claimed recovery, integrity failure enters the same
      fence-bound quarantine path as corrupt checkpoint or journal evidence.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="next-title">
    <h2 id="next-title">What comes next</h2>
    <p>
      The pure barrier planner already validates complete result coverage,
      applies updates to the exact pinned reducer in stable <code>NodeId</code>
      order, resolves continue/route/wait/terminal control, and creates a successor
      checkpoint intent. The next runtime slice will resolve the executable schema/reducer
      registries and drive that intent through the existing atomic PostgreSQL barrier
      commit. Loop, subgraph, and full Agent ergonomics remain later milestones.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
